# 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — will an agent follow this YAML, and what layout serves it

## Answer

**No — not reliably as a gate, though it is a fine readable annex.** The draft keeps every word but breaks the **unit** of a requirement: the level sits in a clause map, the check sits in a group string, and the open choice sits with nobody, so a reader can meet a requirement and still not know what it must do, what fails it, or whom to ask. Keep the three categories, the group ids, the unchanged `text`, the `basis` codes and the four level definitions; change A–D as below, and conditionally — if only an LLM reads the file, B and D are the high-value changes; if a script will parse it, A and C come first.

**Keep.** The spine is right: the categories name the ground — "it is written, it is kept true, and it is read" — which is the correct way to organize rules for a reader who arrives in one of those three situations (my #19). Keep the word-for-word requirements and the `basis` block; the COO's round trip (the script that carried the words over) is worth keeping as a regression check.

**A — Level per clause.** Change, but only for the requirements that already hold clauses at different levels (W1.1, W1.3, W2.1, W2.2, W3.3, W3.5, W4.1, W5.3, W6.1, W6.3, K6.1, K6.3, Rd2.3, Rd3.1, Rd4.5). Give each such clause its own id and its own `level`; leave the single-level requirements with one scalar `level`. Why: today a `level` key is a paraphrase of a sentence in `text`, so the reader must match two texts by meaning and the two copies can drift. Cost: it rewrites the prose the round trip certified, so that check must be re-run.

**B — Where `fails_if` sits.** Change to one `fails_if` per requirement, plus a `check:` field naming the runner (`lint`, `git`, `reviewer`). Why: a group check is a hard failure that does not sit on the requirement it judges, and in this draft it contradicts the levels — W2's group `fails_if` fails on a `kind` value while W2.1 and W2.2 are `guidance`/`SHOULD`; W4's fails on a missing `changed:` line while W4.2 is `SHOULD`; and the file itself says a SHOULD "may be skipped with a written reason." A fail must be on the same unit as the level it enforces. Cost: repetition, and moving a check does not create the reviewer the judged checks lack.

**C — Links.** Change: add a `links:` list of requirement ids per requirement (and per group), while keeping the inline `(K4.1)` mention for human readers. Why: the cross-references are load-bearing — W4.1 carries the Rd5.3 exception, W1.1 leans on W3.6, K4.3 and W6.3 depend on each other — and an agent that must scan prose to resolve them can skip them. Cost: a second copy of each reference; add a lint that every id in `links:` resolves and every inline `(X)` appears in `links:`.

**D — Who decides.** Change: add `who_decides` only where the text leaves a real choice open, and write `none` where it does not. Why: the house rule (which I see in the facts) is to name who decides each open choice; without it an agent facing a MUST it cannot settle will settle it itself — Rd5.3 is the model, where the text already says EJ decides whether the entry or the code changes. Cost: it invites readers to treat every clause as negotiable, so keep the vocabulary closed and `none` explicit.

